OL,3,AWhat weapon could the US have put into
OL,4,Aspace?
OL,5,
OL,6,FThe announcement by the United States
OL,7,Fthat it has deployed a space weapon in
OL,8,FEarth's orbit has raised questions
OL,9,Fabout what weaponry is actually in
OL,10,Fspace, and what role it could play in
OL,11,Fefforts by great powers to dominate
OL,12,Fthat arena.
OL,14,FWashington has given away little in its
OL,15,Fannouncement. Secretary of the Air
OL,16,FForce Troy Meink said the "on orbit"
OL,17,Fweapon was necessary to safeguard US
OL,18,Fforces against hostile enemy action,
OL,19,Fbut gave no details about the weapon's
OL,20,Fcapabilities or when it was put into
OL,21,Forbit.
OL,23,APub: 15-SepQppppppppppppppppppppppG1/2
OL,24,ANext PageB Nat NewsC Nat TVF Clean Feed
FL,316,300,200,140,0,100
PN,31502
SC,0002
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F315A1789541740
OL,1,W5j 5Q7kjs15j 5ws
OL,2,W//%*// //%Q%**,$-.,%,. (,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,AWhat weapon could the US have put into
OL,4,Aspace?
OL,5,
OL,6,FSo what could this weapon be, and what
OL,7,Fis the wider picture like for military
OL,8,Fdevelopments in space? Who else could
OL,9,Fhave similar weaponry?
OL,11,FSpace has been a militarised domain
OL,12,Fsince the start of the space age - in
OL,13,Fthe late 1960s, the Soviet Union
OL,14,Fdeployed weapons up there. The
OL,15,FIstrebitel Sputnikov (IS; Satellite
OL,16,FDestroyer) programme was made up of
OL,17,Fsatellites that were designed to
OL,18,Frelease projectiles that detonated,
OL,19,Freleasing shrapnel into the path of
OL,20,Fanother (usually American) satellite.
OL,23,APub: 15-SepQppppppppppppppppppppppG2/2
